Subject: the elements before a tuple pattern's spread bind what they were handed

Issue: ubugeeei-prod/uf#300
Upstream: not filed with facebook/flow yet
Submodule: 81b0c2a3dd591c66c51167aac341d851932bd9c5

The leading `infer` of a conditional type's tuple pattern came out as `mixed`
-- printed as `unknown` -- as soon as the pattern had a spread in it, and the
same pattern with no spread came out right:

    type First<P> = P extends [infer K, ...infer Rest] ? K : empty;
    type Only<P> = P extends [infer K] ? K : empty;
    declare const head: First<["address", "city"]>;
    declare const one: Only<["address"]>;
    // First: unknown.  Only: "address".

That does not fail, it answers wrongly, which is what makes it worth a patch:
the recursion it blocks -- `T extends [infer H, ...infer R] ? ... : NoForm<R>`
-- returns `true` for `[string, FormData]`, so anything built on it is silently
inverted rather than rejected. `packages/router/action.js` says why
`ActionArguments` cannot hold a call to at most one form because of it.

A pattern with a spread in it is not built as a tuple type at all.
`flow_js_utils::mk_tuple_type_with_env` turns it into an `EvalT` whose base is
the *spread's* type and whose `SpreadTupleType` destructor carries the elements
before it; the no-spread branch of the same function builds a real `TupleAT`.
Subtyping a tuple against a `TupleAT` decomposes element-wise and each `infer`
gets its element as a lower bound. Against the destructor nothing decomposes:
evaluating it blocks on the unresolved spread variable, so the speculative
subtyping in `implicit_instantiation::solve_conditional_type_targs` succeeds
against nothing and the leading `infer` is pinned with no bounds at all --
`on_missing_bounds` then falls back to the tparam's declared bound, and an
`infer` with no annotation is declared `mixed`.

The *spread* variable is already solved, by the reverse of that destructor in
`t_of_use_t`: it takes the tuple the destructor's output was handed and slices
it with `ArrRestT` from the number of elements before the spread. That reverse
is where the elements before the spread were missing, and this patch is that
one addition -- element `i` of the tuple the output was handed flows into
element `i` of the pattern, which is the flow the no-spread branch gets for
free. A lower bound only, the direction the `TupleAT` decomposition takes for a
pattern that only reads its elements, and only when the tuple has elements to
give: a plain `Array<T>` is left alone rather than guessed at, so the half of
#300 that is about an array literal reaching a conditional as an array is
deliberately untouched by this and stays filed.

Two limits it does not lift, both measured, both the same shape as before the
patch rather than made worse by it:

  * arity is still not checked, so `[X, Y, ...R]` still matches a one-element
    tuple. It used to bind `X` as `unknown` there and now binds it as that
    element; neither is the `empty` branch it should have taken. That is the
    destructor never resolving, not the elements never binding.
  * a spread that is not last -- `[...infer R, infer L]` -- is refused by the
    reverse before this code, and still is.

Measured against `flow` itself before it was written: `flow-bin@0.330.0`, the
release this submodule commit is pinned to, reports the same diagnostics as an
unpatched `uf check` through `flow check` -- `unknown` for the leading `infer`
in every case above, and the same words. The port is faithful here and Flow's
typing rule is what is wrong, which is why this is a stopgap -- delete it when
`facebook/flow` takes the fix and the submodule is bumped past it.

